{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `61504cfa`, for DD-1: one digest for each act.\n\nThe numbers decide it. Grow Room has 30 kinds of act, and a digest\nfor the scope makes a change of one kind cost the open forms of the\n29 others. A digest for the act limits the cost to the kind that\nchanged, and to the kinds that share the changed content of\nsection 10. Those kinds did change in meaning, so that cost is\nright.\n\nConditions for the builder source of the descriptor:\n\n1. The digest of an act covers the content that section 10 lists\n   for that act, from the definition bytes. It covers no version\n   text, no name of the application, no path and no other act.\n2. Two tests with real definitions. A version-only release keeps\n   the digest of each act. A change of one act changes the digest\n   of that act and of no act that shares no content with it.\n3. A draft row and a retained authorization each hold the digest\n   of their own act. The field of section 7.1 stays one digest.\n4. Step E4 landed an option that takes one digest for the\n   instance. State in the proposal how the instance gets the\n   digest of an act after DD-1, and what changes for a caller of\n   the option. Prefer one lookup by the kind of the act.\n5. State which callers pass a digest today in a deployed Worker or\n   a shipped package, and with what value. A retained\n   authorization that holds an old value stops with\n   `descriptor-mismatch` at its next version, so I need to know if\n   one can exist before the recipe changes.\n\nDD-1 stays behind C4. The seventh amendment of the note takes the\nrule into section 10.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}